byron@cc.gatech.edu (Byron A Jeff) wrote:
>Well you can. And every class, even the wombats, should take advantage of
>it. So how do you get it?

I would strongly dispute the validity of the protection racket for
wombats; the protection racket requires a great deal of attention to
detail that goes against what seems to be the point of playing a wombat,
which is that the wombats are very straightforward to play.

A valkyrie in standard "kill everything that you sanely can" mode can
reach the minetown without any special precautions often enough that any
other approach seems at best gilding the lily and at worst downright
misguided.

OTOH, it's close to the least bad plan for a healer (who starts with an
enormous pile of MONEY, some healing potions, a wand suitable for evading
enemies with, and very little else of note), and reasonable for an
archaeologist or tourist.

>You get protection from the Minetown priest. It costs about $4000 and a bit
>of time and trouble. But virtually every time I pull it off, I get a
>character that can then go deep into the game.

Intrinsic AC 1 costs 2800-4000 gold, on average, for a first-level character.

>The toughest part is going against typical Nethack nature. Pulling this off
>requires passive aggression. Specifically:
>
>1) Let you pet be your protector. Pets get experience for kills. And if you
>allow your let to get all the kills in the early game, they quickly become a
>formidable force in the game.

Yes.  Conversely, some games, when you're going for the racket, your pet
will kill you with a rolling boulder trap, or itself on DL1 with a
falling rock trap.

Also, be careful with your pet when you get to minetown; it might just
decide it fancies some tasty watch captain meat, and the watch captain
has a *much* better AC than it does.

>2) Monster power and experience goes up with your characters experience.
>Ever wonder how you get newts on level one in the early game, but when you
>come back to level one at the end game you're getting fire giants and
>dragons? Well the types of monsters generated is based on your characters XL.
>So if you keep the XL low, then the danger/power of the monsters generated
>remains low too. Dungeon level factors in also.

Indeed.  Be prepared for the possibility that the minetown will be at a
depth of 10 (mine entrance on 5, minetown on 5th level of mines); if
this happens, you have a materially significant risk of getting attacked
by giant ants or killer bees, both of which are really quite nasty for a
first-level character. (I'm trying to remember whether orcs of various
sorts acn get generated in the mines; if they can, you also have to
watch out for Mordor orcs and Uruk-hai, who both carry poisoned arrows.)

>3) The cost of protection is proportional to experience level. So it's
>cheapest when you're XL 1.
>
>So all three of these tendencies means that you want to kill as little as
>possible in the early game. So the pick axe or the bullwhip shouldn't
>matter to very much in the early game.
>
>So here's what you do.
>
>1) Let your pet kill (and eat) everything. You stick close to your pet
>and pray when you are weak. Eat the lichen and whatever other veggies
>or food rations you may come across.
>
>2) Use the game's shop system to get money. Of course an Arch can simply 
>mine for money, but the downside is that it'll cost you extra nurition
>to do so. Generally you'll get 1 or 2 shops in the first 4 levels of the
>main dungeon,

Really?  It's unremarkable to get none at all.

>and of course minetown itself will have a bunch of shops.

Usually.

>The game allows you to sell stuff to the shopkeep. It also allows pets
>to steal stuff from out of shops. And the current game allows for
>credit cloning, though most players believe it's a form of cheating.
>The bottom line is that with some patience and your pet you can clean
>out a shop of both items and money. Enough money to pay the priest.

If you've been letting your pet get ahead of you, it takes a *lot* of
patience to clean out a shop, because you probably have no treats to
train it with.

>3) AC is still critical. Wear any armor you come across as long as it's
>not cursed. You may even want to take a chance on a cursed item if the
>reward is great enough. For example I'd probably wear cursed banded mail
>early in the game. Even at -1 it gets you down to AC 3. Negative AC is
>important because not only does it make you harder to hit, but the
>actual damage inflicted is reduced. Finally if you don't know if armor
>is cursed or not, then back your pet into a blocked hallway (closed
>door or not yet searched corridor, and drop the item in question. If
>you get reluctance from the pet to step on it, it's cursed. Otherwise
>if you get nothing, or the pet picks it up, then it's at least uncursed
>and you should go ahead and wear it.

Exception: Do not let yourself become burdened just to get more AC; being
burdened *and* first level will get you killed, even with a good AC.

>4) Get to the minetown priest, and get your naked AC to at least 1,
